A||||7||Adrift||What tool is needed to remove the breakline?||||1||
Z||000000||Adrift||12-09-2001||01:51 PM||||I want to pull the rubber rear lines off my car to replace with s.s. braided lines, but I can't figure out what tool to use to get the old rubber line off of the steel line because there is so much rust and corrosion - what tool do you use for this?<P>(And I know is BRAKE not break I dont know why I keep spelling it like that!)<p>[This message has been edited by Adrift (edited 12-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierospeeder||12-09-2001||02:43 PM||||your suppose to use a flare wrench, its just like a open end wrench but it has an additional side to hold the nut.<P>I would spray the fitting with wd40 or pb blaster and try to rub the rust off.  So you can get a tigher fit, without stripping.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||12-09-2001||07:21 PM||||use a Line Wrench. it looks like a box end with a slot on one side to get it on over the brake line. Buy a good one, not cheap or itll slip and round off corners.||||reg||1
Z||000003||88-DOHC||12-09-2001||08:17 PM||||I just did this recently on my car.  One thing I would like to add. Even though I had the correct wrench, one of the cars I did this to, the lines were so tight that the wrench was stripping the fitting.  Ended up having to basicly make a shim out of one of those metal "gap" testers to get a tight enough grip around the fitting. I had to literally pound the wrench onto the fitting.  This was on the rear of the car, after my friend had screwed up the fronts so bad that he gave up and let me do the rest. We ended up having to cut the front ones and re-flare the lines. Not fun.||||reg||1
Z||000004||SKIDMARK||12-09-2001||08:30 PM||||I hate to be a doomsayer but I would consider replacing the steel brake lines if they are that rusty. Although it would cost some money, it would be worth it to get the rusty lines & fittings replaced. I have done brake lines on many cars and have found that it's usually not worth the fight to save old rusty parts. The steel braded hoses are a good idea but first you need dependable steel lines.<BR>If the lines & fittings aren't that bad and one of the above metioned wrenches don't work, you can usually get away with a small set of vise grip piers but don't crush the fitting. Use the real jagged teeth in the middle of the jaw. A little heat from a torch might help too. <BR>Check your bleeders at the calipers before you start. If they are no good, you'll have no way to remove the air from the system once you replace the hoses.<BR>Good luck and let us know how it turns out.<P>------------------<BR>White '85 4cyl coupe<BR>Black '86 v-6 SE<BR>'81 CB900F Super Sport||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jncomutt||12-09-2001||09:32 PM||||Everyone keeps talkin about replacing the steel line but where do I get them?  I know that the driverside rear caliper gets very little fluid when the brake is depressed, and that i have to change the metal line.  (I switched to ss lines, but cudn't get this one to bleed for the life of me.)  So either its crimped or clogged.  I couldn't spot where it was visually so I just want to replace the entire line.. But how??<P>BTW, the pass. side rear flows great.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierospeeder||12-10-2001||06:07 AM||||most car stores carry brake lines for a few dollars.<P>They are already flared and have fittings on them.||||reg||1
Z||000007||GTDude||12-12-2001||12:31 PM||||I too suggest replacing the steel lines, but you will prob need a pair of vice grip pliers to ever get them loose...from my experience.<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
